              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The traditional notion of
            %em recursion
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            is a way of defining a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            by specifying the image of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            (the “initial value”) along with a way to obtain each
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successive
            value from the previous one(s).
            %br>/
            The study of functions on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            which can or cannot be defined using only recursion –
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursive+functions"} recursive functions
            – is the topic of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computability+theory"} computability theory
            and has deep connections with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
          %p
            More generally, recursion is a way of defining a function on any mathematical object which is “defined
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductively
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            are characterized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
          %p
            Recursion is formalized in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            by the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            (and the corresponding
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rule"} elimination rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            by the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra+of+an+endofunctor"} initial algebra of an endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(X) \to X
            determines a collection of
            %em constructors
            and the
            %em recursion principle
            is the statement that there is a (unique) morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to X
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            such structure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A) \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %em recursively defined function
          %p
            Viewed from just a slightly different angle, this state of affairs is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction+principle"} induction principle
            on non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"}> dependent types
            \.
          %h2#examples Examples
          .un_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              In the theory of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x + y
              recursively in terms of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
              operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              as follows:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x + 0 = x
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x + s(y) = s(x + y)
            %p
              The definition above is taken as an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            %p
              More generally, given a (parametrizable)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              with the above properties.
          %h2#recursion_theorem Recursion theorem
          %h3#in_general In general
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇝
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightsquigarrow
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded relation
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon X \times Y \times \mathcal{P}(Z) \to Z
              be a given function. Then, there is a unique function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Z
              satisfying
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f (x', y) = h (x', y, S)
            %p
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Z
                    %mo :
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⇝
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \{ z \in Z : \exists x . \, ( x \rightsquigarrow x' \wedge z = f (x, y) ) \}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p By the principle of well-founded induction. (Details to be added.)
          %h3#for_a_natural_numbers_object For a natural numbers object
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              be a (parametrizable)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
              in a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} category with finite products
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\colon 1 \to \mathbb{N}
              and successor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\colon \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0\colon Y \to Z
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon \mathbb{N} \times Y \times Z \to Z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon \mathbb{N} \times Y \to Z
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0, -) = f_0
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(s(x), y) = h(s(x), y, f(x, y))
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon \mathbb{N} \times Y \to \mathbb{N}
              is the first projection and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\colon \mathbb{N} \times Y \to Y
              is the second projection.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Recall that the universal property for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              states that for data
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_0\colon Y \to A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\colon Y \times A \to A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon \mathbb{N} \times Y \to A
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \circ (0 \times \mathrm{id}_Y) = g_0
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \circ (s \times \mathrm{id}_Y) = k \circ u
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \mathbb{N} \times Z
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_0 = 0 \times f_0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(y, n, z) = \langle n, h(n, y, z) \rangle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\colon Y \times \mathbb{N} \times Z \to \mathbb{N}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\colon Y \times \mathbb{N} \times Z \to Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\colon Y \times \mathbb{N} \times Z \to Z
              are the obvious projections. The
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              we seek is then obtained as
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2 \circ u
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2 : \mathbb{N} \times Z \to Z
              is the second projection.
